    "result_text": "Done. The build is green.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec: reject `POST /api/owners` (and `PUT /api/owners/{ownerId}`) with **400** when the owner's email domain is a disposable domain (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`).\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions and single-responsibility rule, I kept the disposable-domain concern separate from the existing syntactic email check (`NormalizeOwnerEmail`) and added four small pieces:\n\n1. **`util/DisposableEmailDomains.java`** \u2014 the blocklist and a case-insensitive `isBlocked(email)`, mirroring the existing `Postcodes`/`CityLocality` util style. Owns the \"what domains are disposable\" knowledge; reusable.\n\n2. **`function/owner/ValidateOwnerEmailDomain.java`** \u2014 a `Validate<E>` step that reads the shared `OwnerFieldsDto` via `@Val`, skips absent/blank email (optional field), and throws the checked exception when blocked.\n\n3. **`escalation/DisposableEmailDomainException.java`** + **`DisposableEmailDomainExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 a checked exception and a handler that responds 400 with a `ProblemDetail`, exactly matching the shape of the sibling `InvalidEmail`/`InvalidPostcode` escalations.\n\n4. Wiring: the new `validateEmailDomain` step is chained right after `normalizeEmail` in both `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml` (so it runs after the address is validated and lower-cased), plus the escalation YAML routing the exception to the handler.\n\nThe new `errorRejectsDisposableDomain` acceptance test and the full suite pass.",
